      "This monograph elaborates an integrated diagnosis of the structural vulnerabilities and the resilience potential of the Romanian society in the face of the informational and cognitive warfare operations carried out by the Russian Federation. In contemporary allied doctrine developed by NATO's Supreme Allied Command Transformation, cognitive space is defined as the sixth operational domain, alongside land, sea, air, space and cyber (Claverie, Baptiste & du Cluzel, 2022; du Cluzel, 2020). Unlike classical information warfare, which aims to control data flows and targeted persuasion, cognitive warfare aims to alter the architecture of reality processing and erode trust in democratic institutions. Its ultimate objective is to compromise ethical discernment and paralyze a nation's decision-making capacity without triggering the threshold of Article 5 of the North Atlantic Treaty. The present analysis abandons any metaphor external to the investigated scientific fields and exclusively uses the theoretical and conceptual repertoire of institutional sociology, cognitive neuroscience, social cybernetics, semiotics of culture and post-Jungian analytical psychology.",
